  "slug": "genome-chromosome-level-fb51",
  "summary": "Assembly contiguity now pairs with analyses of transposons, organellar genomes and gene loss in plant-associated lineages.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of chromosome-level or organelle-resolved genome data to relate genome architecture to heterotrophic or symbiotic biology. The Gastrodia elata chromosome-level genome and 12-accession pan-genome associate mycoheterotrophy with photosynthesis-gene loss and GAFP expansion, and report no fungal horizontal gene transfer. The Starship preprint in Pyricularia oryzae compares transferability, mutability and epigenetic marks across transposon compartments, and releases an updated stargraph. The study of 45 Orobanchaceae mitogenomes finds that recombination and RNA-mediated retroprocessing drive mitochondrial evolution in heterotrophic plants. What is new is the pairing of assembly-scale data with mechanistic analyses of mobile elements and gene loss. The Pyricularia and broomrape items align most directly with this thread, while the orchid study extends it through pan-genomic comparison.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-01-31"
}
